To overcome the challenges in pure polymeric and inorganic membranes, mixed matrix membranes (MMMs) have been proposed by dispersing organic/inorganic materials as fillers into polymeric materials as matrixes, hoping to synergistically combine the good processability of polymers and excellent separation performance of fillers. Nevertheless, it is a challenging task to fabricate MMMs with superior separation performance, good processability, high mechanical strength, low cost, and long-term stability, which represent basic standards for MMMs to be commercialized for industrial applications. Since the first exploration of MMMs for gas separation in 1970s by incorporating 5A zeolite into a rubbery polymer,[9] a large number of organic/inorganic materials have been explored as potential fillers, including various zeolites, metal oxides, activated carbon, carbon molecular sieves, and mesoporous silica.[10] However, these traditional fillers often result in nonselective voids or defects in MMMs originating from the poor polymer–filler compatibility, leading to a significant loss of membrane selectivity.[11] With the rapid development of chemistry and materials science, recent studies have shifted toward the design and application of advanced porous materials, such as MOFs, covalent organic frameworks (COFs), porous organic cages (POCs), etc., for the preparation of defect-free MMMs with decent separation performance (Figure 1).[12] Based on the International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry (IUPAC) recommendations, porous materials should have shape-persistent free-volume elements (e.g., pores). Moreover, they can be further classified as microporous (pore width smaller than \(2~\mathrm{nm}\)), mesoporous (pore width between 2 and \(50~\mathrm{nm}\)), and macroporous (pore width larger than \(50~\mathrm{nm}\)) materials depending on their pore size.[13] For gas and liquid separations, microporous materials are highly desired owing to their small pore size close to the kinetic diameters of gas and liquid molecules, which is beneficial for achieving size-selective separations in the membranes. Contrary to conventional porous materials such as zeolites, activated carbon, carbon nanotubes, and mesoporous silica, these advanced porous fillers exhibit rich chemical functionality and tunable pore structures, largely broadening the scope of potential fillers in MMMs. Very recently, several reviews have covered the development of MMMs with some advanced porous fillers, which mostly focused on MOF, especially zeolitic imidazolate framework (ZIF), based MMMs for gas and liquid separations.[14] Nevertheless, as far as we know, only limited reviews have provided dedicated insights regarding the choice and design of other emerging advanced porous fillers, including COFs and POCs, for the fabrication of MMMs with promising separation performance.[10,15] Considering the rapid development of the research in this field, novel filler design strategies such as 2D COF fillers and postsynthetically modified cage compounds have been proposed to mitigate the polymer–filler compatibility challenges in MMMs. Moreover, the scarcity of an overview on MMMs with these emerging advanced porous fillers (e.g., COFs) for liquid separations prompts us to offer a timely summary in this field, hoping to shed lights on the preparation of more advanced membranes for liquid separation. Herein, we first provide a comprehensive

