# 2. Choices of Advanced Porous Materials

# 2.1. MOFs

MOFs are hybrid porous materials composed of inorganic nodes connected by polytopic organic linkers via coordination bonds.[16] Due to their high porosity, tunable pore architectures, and rich chemical functionalities, MOFs have shown great promise for diverse applications, including gas storage and separation, thin film devices, catalysis, chemical sensing, proton conduction, and biomedical imaging.[16,17] Compared to their inorganic counterparts, e.g., zeolites, MOFs are more promising fillers in MMMs. The high affinity between organic linkers in MOFs and polymer chains in the matrix largely enhances polymer–filler interactions, mitigating the formation of nonselective voids or defects at the interface. Moreover, the judicious design of MOF fillers with functional groups, such as \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), \(-\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}\), and \(-\mathrm{OH}\) groups, provides a powerful toolbox to further improve polymer–filler compatibility in MMMs. MOFs with large pores \((>1\ \mathrm{nm})\) might allow

the partial penetration of polymer chains to promote MOF dispersion in the polymer matrix and strengthen the adhesion between two phases. Since the first report of MOF-based MMMs in 2004,[18] extensive studies have been performed in this field to prepare high-performance MMMs. A large number of MOFs have been chosen as potential fillers and among them, the most typical examples include ZIFs, copper-based MOFs (Cu-MOFs), Materials Institute Lavoisier (MIL) series, MOF-74 series, and University of Oslo-66 (UiO-66) series (Figure 2).

# 2.1.1. ZIFs

ZIFs are a sub-family of MOFs composed of tetrahedrally coordinated metal cations, such as \(\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\), bridged by imidazolate (Im) and its derivative linkers to form hybrid frameworks with zeolite-like topologies. ZIFs have been extensively investigated as membrane materials due to their tunable pore structures, diverse chemical functionalities, and exceptional chemical resistance toward neutral and weakly basic aqueous solutions and organic solvents.[12a] A series of ZIFs with ultrasmall aperture sizes including ZIF-7 (3.0 Å), ZIF-8 (3.4 Å), ZIF-22 (3.2 Å), and ZIF-90 (3.5 Å) have been processed into pure polycrystalline membranes for ultrafast selective permeation of gases, solvents, and ions.[19] ZIFs are gaining increasing attention as promising fillers in MMMs due to their molecular sieving effects, good compatibility with the polymer matrix, and facile synthesis process. For example, the crystallographic pore aperture of ZIF-8 falls between the kinetic

diameters of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) (3.3 Å) and \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) (3.68 Å). Theoretically, ZIF-8 should exhibit a stringent molecular sieving capability for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\) separation by allowing the transport of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) while blocking the passage of \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\). However, recent studies indicate that MMMs with ZIF-8 only exhibit a moderate \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) selectivity originating from the flexible pore structure of ZIF-8 nanocrystals.[20] In fact, experimental results demonstrate that large molecules, such as \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}\) (diameter: 3.7 Å), \(n\)-octane (diameter: 4.3 Å), and caffeine (6.1 × 7.6 Å), can still freely move in and out of ZIF-8 cavities, further confirming this structural flexibility.[14d,21] To achieve the molecular sieving property of ZIF-8 for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) separations, Ban et al. finely tuned the effective cage size of ZIF-8 by incorporating a room-temperature ionic liquid (RTIL) as the cavity occupant in an in situ ionothermal synthesis (Figure 3a).[22] The RTIL not only functions as the cavity occupant, but also possesses high affinity to \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), significantly improving the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption capacity of original ZIF-8 fillers. Compared to conventional inorganic fillers such as zeolites, ZIF-8 nanocrystals show better compatibility with the polymer
