# 2.1.2. Cu-MOFs

\(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) (also known as HKUST-1, HKUST stands for Hong Kong University of Science and Technology) and CuBDC (copper 1,4-benzene dicarboxylate) are the two most typical Cu-MOFs as fillers in MMMs. \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) is assembled from di-copper paddlewheel secondary building units (SBUs) and benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylate (BTC) linkers, forming a 3D framework with an intersecting 3D channel system.[31] \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) possesses channels with a size of \(9\,\mathrm{\AA}\) allowing the fast permeation of guests and triangular windows with a size of \(3.5\,\mathrm{\AA}\) suitable for molecular sieving separation. On the contrary, CuBDC has a 2D layered structure with each layer being a porous grid consisting of di-copper paddlewheel SBUs and 1,4-benzene dicarboxylate linkers. The crystallographic pore size of CuBDC grid is \(5.2\mathrm{~\AA~}\) , which can enable the discriminative diffusion of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) (kinetic diameter \(3.8\mathrm{~\AA~}\) ) over larger gas molecules, such as \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) (kinetic diameter \(3.64\,\mathrm{~\AA~}\) ) and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) (kinetic diameter \(3.8\,\mathrm{~\AA~}\) ). Interestingly, the removal of guest molecules in these two MOFs during activation process can expose the coordinatively unsaturated copper sites, leading to stronger interactions between Cu-MOFs and guests. In this instance, \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) exhibits high hydrophilicity in the presence of moisture due to strong interactions between unsaturated copper sites and water molecules,[32] which in turn boosts its research in terms of pervaporation and desalination.[33] So far, \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) crystals have been explored as promising fillers in various kinds of polymers, including polyimide (PI, e.g., Matrimid),[34] polysulfone (PSf),[35] poly(dimethylsiloxane) (PDMS),[33a] poly(2,6-dimethyl-1,4-phenylene oxide),[36] etc. Contrary to bulky \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) crystals, CuBDC can be facilely prepared in 2D nanosheet morphology with a bottom-up method, featuring aspect ratios of higher than 20.[37] CuBDC nanosheets not only exhibit good polymer–filler compatibility due to their large contact area with the polymer matrix, but also facilitate the discriminative permeation of guests through membranes.[38] However, it is crucial to properly orientate CuBDC nanosheets to align their 1D pore channels parallel to the gas diffusion path. This can be achieved under certain external forces, such as shear forces and centrifugal forces.[37,39]

