# 2.1.3. MIL Series

MILs refer to those MOFs being developed by Materials Institute Lavoisier.[40] Some of them exhibit interesting properties such as flexibility and high stability, outperforming other MOFs as fillers in MMMs due to their unique structural flexibility upon adsorption/desorption of guests. This so called "breathing effect" is exemplified by the MIL-53 family consisting of trivalent metal cations \(\mathbf{M}^{+}\) \(\mathrm{M}=\mathrm{Al}\) , Cr, Fe, Ga, In) and linear dicarboxylate linkers (e.g., BDC or \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -BDC).[41] MIL-53 MOFs are able to alter their pore size and geometry in response to the adsorption of polar gases (e.g., \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) , \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) ) and hydrocarbons (Figure 4a). This special framework flexibility arouses the interest of membrane community in fabricating novel MMMs with stimuli-responsive characteristics toward

