ane gas permeability. Other MIL series MOFs, such as MIL-96 (Al),[45] MIL-101(Cr),[46] \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) –MIL-101(Al),[47] \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) –MIL-101(Cr),[48] and MIL-125 (Ti),[49] have been explored as fillers to fabricate MMMs with good separation performance.

# 2.1.4. MOF-74 Series

MOF-74 series is composed of \(\mathbf{M}^{2+}\) \(\mathrm{~M~}=\mathrm{~M~g~}\) , Mn, Fe, Co, Ni, Cu, or Zn) and 2,5-dioxido-1,4-benzenedicarboxylate \((\mathrm{dobdc}^{4-})\) , possessing 1D hexagonal channels rich of unsaturated metal sites ideal for the selective adsorption of polar gases (e.g., \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) , \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) ) over other nonpolar gases (e.g., \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) , \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) ).[50] One typical example is \(\mathrm{Mg-MOF-74}\) , exhibiting a ratio of the Henry's Law constant for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CH_{4}}\) adsorption that reaches 330.[51] Detailed simulations reveal that the standard adsorption enthalpy values for \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) in this MOF are \(-21\) and \(-47\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol}^{-1}\) , respectively, further confirming its great potential in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capture processes.[52] Moreover, the 1D hexagonal channels of \(\mathrm{Mg-MOF-74}\) have a diameter of \(11\mathrm{~\AA~}\) , which can provide fast guest transport pathways when embedded in the polymer matrix. Bae and Long explored the effect of \(\mathrm{Mg-MOF-74}\) nanoparticles \((\approx\!100\ \mathrm{nm}\) in diameter) on membrane separation performance in both glassy and rubbery polymer matrixes.[53] Interestingly, they observed simultaneously increased gas permeability and selectivity in the glassy polymer matrix differing from the decreased permeability in

the rubbery polymer matrix after adding Mg-MOF-74 fillers. This discrepancy can be ascribed to the penetration of flexible polymer chains of the rubbery matrix, therefore blocking MOF pores in MMMs. On the contrary, rigid chains of the glassy matrix can hardly penetrate into MOF pores, allowing them to be accessible to gases. To take advantages of MOF-74 as fillers one step further, Tien-Binh et al. cross-linked Mg-MOF-74 in situ with polymer of intrinsic microporosity-1 (PIM-1) to fabricate defect-free MMMs.[54] The solution-based cross-linking process effectively facilitates the polymer chain rearrangement on the filler surface and prevents the filler pore blocking. Moreover, Maserati et al. prepared diamine-appended \(\mathrm{Mg}_{2}(\mathrm{dobpc})\) nanorods and explored them as promising fillers in MMMs (Figure 4c).[55] The dynamic uptake and release of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) along the diamine groups in MOFs largely enhance the membrane \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) permeability and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) selectivity. Recent studies indicate that MOF-74 series with good crystallinity, high surface area, and narrow particle size distribution could be massively produced in a continuous flow process, setting the foundation for their successful implementation in industry.[56]

# 2.1.5. UiO-66 Series

Since its first discovery in 2008,[57] UiO-66 has attracted much attention in a wide variety of applications, such as catalysis,[58] separation,[59] drug delivery,[60] and bioimaging,[61] mainly due to its high porosity, rich chemical functionality, and most importantly, excellent stability in water and various organic solvents. The original UiO-66 constructed from \(\mathrm{Zr}_{6}(\upmu_{3}\mathrm{-O})_{4}(\upmu_{3}\mathrm{-}\) \(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{COO})_{12}\) clusters and linear dicarboxylate linkers features a face-centered-cubic crystal structure. It possesses centric octahedral cages (diameter: 11 Å) interconnected to tetrahedral cages (diameter: 8 Å) through triangular windows (size: 6 Å) with a Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area of close to \(1600\,\mathrm{~m}^{2}\,\mathrm{~g}^{-1}\) .[62] A series of UiO-66-type MOFs with different functional groups [e.g., \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) , \(-(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) , \(-(\mathrm{COOH})_{2}\) , \(-(\mathrm{OCH}_{2}\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2}\) , \(-\mathrm{F}_{4}\) , \(-(\mathrm{COOH})_{4}]\) can be easily synthesized in a modulated hydrothermal method, advancing their massive production and commercialization.[63] Extensive studies have demonstrated that \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\) outperforms pristine UiO-66 as MMM fillers owing to the abundant amino groups in the former, which provide better affinity toward guests and stronger interactions with the polymer matrix via hydrogen bonding in MMMs.[64] Venna et al. further modified UiO\(66{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}\) nanoparticles (denote as I) with different functional groups, including phenyl acetyl \(\left(\mathrm{I}_{\mathrm{PA}}\right)\) , decanoyl acetyl \(\left(\mathrm{I}_{\mathrm{C10}}\right)\) , and succinic acid \(\left(\mathrm{I}_{\mathrm{SA}}\right)\) group, and added them into Matrimid to fabricate MMMs for gas separations.[65] They observed that the effective interactions (including \(\pi{-}\pi\) interaction and hydrogen bonding) between MOF fillers and the polymer matrix meet the following order: \(\mathrm{I}_{\mathrm{PA}}>\mathrm{I}>\mathrm{I}_{\mathrm{C10}}>\mathrm{I}_{\mathrm{SA}}\) (Figure 5a). This work provides a powerful toolbox to enhance the polymer–filler compatibility in MMMs. Besides, Tien-Binh et al. utilized chemical cross-linking between \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\) and PIM-1 to fabricate defect-free MMMs with excellent \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) separation performance surpassing the Robeson 2008 upper bounds (Figure 5b).[66] \(\mathrm{UiO-66-NH}_{2}\) nanoparticles can be homogeneously dispersed in the membrane, and their internal pores can
