# 2.2. POFs

POFs are a sub-class of porous materials consisting of pure organic building blocks via strong covalent bonds, demonstrating rigid structures with high surface area, diverse chemical functionality, adjustable pore structures, and good chemical and thermal stability.[71] The skeletons and pores of POFs can be rationally controlled using a topological design approach where their building blocks play crucial roles in determining their final structures. Compared to the inorganic–organic hybrid nature of MOFs, pure organic nature of POFs can endow them with higher affinity to the organic polymer matrix, providing a good solution to cracks or defects aroused by poor polymer–filler compatibility in MMMs. Up till now, a large number of POFs have been developed, which can be classified into two major categories based on their crystallinity: highly crystalline materials, such as COFs; and amorphous materials, such as PIMs, hyper-cross-linked polymers (HCPs), conjugated microporous polymers (CMPs), and porous aromatic frameworks (PAFs).[72] Considering that PIMs are microporous polymers with good processability into free-standing membranes, they will not be a focus here, and interested readers are referred to the literature for details.[73]

Since their first discovery in 2005,[74] COFs have gained great interests owing to their high crystallinity, good porosity, and well-fined structures. To ensure the formation of ordered frameworks, dynamic covalent chemistry has been widely used for COF synthesis, as it allows the reversible bond formation, providing COF systems with a self-correction ability to repair

structural defects.[12b,75] The first generation of COFs involves boron-based linkages, which however have limited resistance toward water, acid, and alcohols.[12b] On the contrary, imine-based COFs show much enhanced stability in these solvents despite at the cost of crystallinity for some systems.[76] Recently, an irreversible enol-to-keto tautomerism process was introduced into imine-based COFs to further improve their stability toward water, acid, and even base.[77] Kang et al. pioneered to fabricate MMMs with exfoliated 2D water-stable COF nanosheets for efficient \( \mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2} \) separation.[78] The good polymer–filler compatibility originating from pure organic nature and 2D morphology of COF nanosheets affords defect-free MMMs with good mechanical strength and gas separation performance. Subsequently, Biswal et al. dispersed two isoreticular COFs with different pore sizes into the tert-butylpolybenzimidazole (PBI–Bul) matrix to fabricate MMMs with excellent mechanical strength and decent separation performance (Figure 6).[79] To further exploit the potential of COFs, Xu et al. adopted a chemically stable COF as filler to prepare MMMs for water purification.[80] MMMs with an ultralow amount (0.2 wt%) of COF fillers can simultaneously improve water flux and pollutant rejection, breaking the traditional trade-off effect in pure polymeric membranes. With the rapid development of materials science and chemistry, it is anticipated that more COFs will be explored for MMM preparation, such as ionic COFs.[81] 3D COFs,[82] etc.

Amorphous POFs resemble their crystalline counterparts in terms of high porosity, good chemical functionality, and tunable pore structures. Moreover, they have excellent chemical stability toward harsh conditions due to their irreversible covalent bonds, which largely broaden their applicable scope. CMPs were first synthesized by Jiang et al. in 2007, through Sonogashira–Hagihara cross-coupling of alkyne and halogen monomers.[83] Contrary to most CMPs that are insoluble powders with irregular morphology, soluble CMPs can be prepared by judiciously choosing monomers that favor the formation of discrete hyperbranched chains instead of extended networks, allowing the preparation of CMP thin films.[84]
