Free-standing CMP membranes can be fabricated on a sacrificial substrate via a layer-by-layer synthetic approach, with the membrane thickness of down to \(8\ \mathrm{nm}\).[85] Nevertheless, until now, CMP-based MMMs are still yet to be explored mainly due to their relatively expensive building blocks and complicated synthetic approaches. As alternatives, HCPs are synthesized from cheap starting materials under mild reaction conditions, which reinforce their large-scale production. HCPs began their scientific journey as fillers in MMMs in 2016 when Lau et al. incorporated a,a′-dichloro-p-xylene (p-DCX), a typical HCP synthesized at room temperature, into a super glassy matrix, poly(1-trimethylsilyl-1-propyne) (PTMSP), to fabricate MMMs for gas separation.[86] The partial penetration of bulky trimethylsilyl groups of PTMSP into pores of p-DCX effectively retards the aging process in MMMs. In a similar manner, Mitra et al. added hyper-cross-linked polystyrene nanoparticles into the highly permeable PIM-1 matrix, largely improving the antiaging ability of resultant MMMs.[87] However, HCPs still face the challenges of low surface area and irregular pore networks, which stimulate the search for materials with ultrahigh surface area and controllable pore architectures. PAFs constructed from self-assembled aromatic rings demonstrate BET surface areas of as high as \(5640\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and adjustable pore sizes ranging from microscale to mesoscale.[88] Polymer chains are also able to penetrate into PAF pores in MMMs, leading to a remarkable cessation in membrane aging.[89]

# 2.3. Porous Molecular Compounds

The judicious selection of suitable porous fillers that can strongly interact with the polymer matrix stimulates the everlasting search for novel porous materials, ranging from purely inorganic zeolites, inorganic–organic MOFs to purely organic

POFs. However, these extended frameworks exhibit poor solubility in common solvents that dissolve polymer materials, which frequently leads to uncontrollable filler dispersion and unavoidable defects in the resultant MMMs. Extensive studies have revealed that fillers with high surface area to volume ratios, e.g., nanoparticles and nanosheets, are beneficial for enhancing interfacial interactions owing to their large contact area with the polymer matrix.[11c,37,64] Nevertheless, it is challenging to synthesize these well-dispersed nanoparticles or nanosheets with uniform sizes suitable for membrane preparation. As alternatives, porous molecular compounds, including POCs and metal–organic cages (MOCs), with good solubility as well as processability, can function as ideal fillers in MMMs to achieve homogeneous filler dispersion and excellent polymer–filler compatibility at a molecular level.

# 2.3.1. POCs

Since their first discovery in 2009 by Tozawa et al.,[90] POCs have aroused great interests as a novel member of the porous materials family owing to their excellent solubility in various solvents. Similar to their porous analogues, e.g., MOFs and COFs, POCs also possess attractive properties in terms of their molecular design, such as adjustable pore architectures and rich chemical functionality. The BET surface area of POCs can reach as high as \(3700\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\), highlighting their great potential in gas storage and separation.[91] Impressively, liquids with permanent porosity can be easily prepared by dissolving POCs in solvents which are too large to penetrate into the cage cavities.[92] These intriguing properties of POCs boost their exploration as promising fillers in MMMs. As revealed by computational simulations, MMMs composed of POCs with sizes falling in the range of the kinetic diameter of target
