# 2.3.2. MOCs

MOCs (also known as metal–organic polyhedra, MOP) are discrete metal–organic assemblies built from metal nodes and organic linkers via coordination bonds.[12d,95] MOCs possess shape-persistent pores and well-defined molecular size and shape, exhibiting good solubility in various solvents. As a result, it is anticipated that MOCs can disperse perfectly in the polymer

matrix and help to construct defect-free MMMs. For example, MOP-18, a typical MOC decorated by long alkyl chains,[96] has hydrophilic cavities (diameter: \(13.8\mathrm{~\AA~}\) ) interconnected by triangular windows (size: \(3.8\mathrm{~\AA~}\) ) and square windows (size: \(6.6\mathrm{~\AA~}\) ). MOP-18 has been investigated as synthetic ion channels to enable the fine control on the transport of proton and alkali-metal ions across lipid membranes.[97] Perez et al. incorporated up to \(80\,\mathrm{wt}\%\) of MOP-18 into Matrimid to fabricate MMMs with excellent polymer–filler compatibility.[98] These MMMs show no sign of polymer rigidification at the interface. The strong polymer–filler affinity leads to enhanced membrane mechanical strength and antiplasticization performance. Besides, the introduction of functional groups that preferentially interact with specific guests into MOCs proves to be effective in further improving their intrinsic gas permeation performance. For instance, Ma et al. incorporated a soluble MOC, \(\mathrm{Na}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{18}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Cu}_{24}(5\mathrm{-}\) \(\mathrm{SO}_{3^{-}}1,3\mathrm{-}\mathrm{BDC})_{24}(\mathrm{S})_{24}]\cdot x\mathrm{S}\) \(\mathrm{~\textit~{~\textcent~}~}=\) methanol/N,N-dimethylacetamide), into PSf to fabricate MMMs for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) separation.[99] This MOC possesses polar \(-\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{Na}\) groups that can interact more strongly with \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) than \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) due to the high polarizability and quadrupole moment of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) , leading to a substantial increase in the membrane \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) selectivity. On the contrary, when substituting these \(-\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{Na}\) groups with less polar \(-\mathrm{OH}\) groups, MOCs exhibit much weaker capability in improving the membrane selectivity and tend to agglomerate in the membrane even at a loading of only \(8\,\mathrm{wt}\%\) . In a similar manner, Zhao et al.
synthesized three isostructural functionalized MOP–X \(\left[\mathrm{X}=\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{Na}_{n}\mathrm{H}_{m}\right.\) \(\left.\left(n+m=1\right)\right.\), \(\left.\left(\mathrm{OH}\right.\), or \(t\mathrm{Bu}\right]\), and investigated their performance in MMMs toward aromatic hydrocarbons recovery (Figure 8c).[100] Simulation results reveal that the overall binding energies between functional groups in MOCs and toluene molecules follow the sequence of \(\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{Na}_{n}\mathrm{H}_{m}>\mathrm{OH}>t\mathrm{Bu}\), demonstrating that stronger polar groups possess stronger affinity toward toluene molecules. Other MOCs, such as M–MOP (M. Cu, Fe, and Pd),[101] \(\mathrm{EG}_{3}\mathrm{MOP}\),[102] and MOP–tBu,[103] have also been explored as good fillers in MMMs for separation purposes. To sum up, the development of MOC-based MMMs is still in its infancy, and it is anticipated that novel MOC design and chemical crosslinking between MOCs and the polymer matrix may function as powerful tools to prepare MMMs with superior separation performance in the foreseeable future.

To make a brief summary, the abovementioned advanced porous materials can offer many opportunities in the design and fabrication of high-performance membranes with huge potentials in industrial applications. Their rich chemical functionality and good affinity to the polymer matrix synergistically enhance the polymer–filler compatibility in MMMs, helping to realize their intrinsic separation properties. However, chemical stability still remains a big concern for some advanced porous fillers, such as Cu-based MOFs and MOCs, MOF-74 series, imine-based COFs, POCs, etc. For example, \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}\) was reported to experience a BET surface area decrease from 1340 to \(647\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) after immersion in water at \(323\;\mathrm{K}\) for \(24\;\mathrm{h}\), indicating a serious crystallinity loss and material decomposition upon direct contact with water.[32] It is anticipated that water-stable MOFs, especially UiO-66 series, will deserve more attention in future research. As for imine-based COFs and POCs, their chemical stability can be improved by transforming imine linkages into amine or amide linkages via an irreversible chemical conversion route.[104] It is therefore of significance to choose appropriate fillers with good chemical stability, which sets the foundation of the long term stability of membranes under realistic operation conditions.

# 3. Novel Trends in MMMs

# 3.1. 2D Fillers

It is widely accepted that both the structure and morphology of fillers have pronounced influence on the membrane separation performance.[44,105] As demonstrated in the typical example of CuBDC nanosheet-based MMMs, the incorporation of 2D MOF fillers can largely improve the membrane \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) selectivity owing to the increase of gas diffusion resistance through size-selective channels in MOF nanosheets.[37] Tomographic focused

