ion beam scanning electron microscopy (FIB-SEM) directly proves that the spatial occupation of 2D MOF nanosheets in the membrane cross-section perpendicular to the gas flux largely exceeds their bulky isotropic crystals, thus eliminating unselective permeation pathways and improving the efficiency of molecular discrimination (Figure 8a). It is worth mentioning that an unexpected and greatly desired increase in separation selectivity with pressure was observed in MMMs containing CuBDC nanosheets, opposite to the decrease trend observed in other membranes, which can be ascribed to the intrinsic separation performance of 2D MOF fillers (Figure 8b). Subsequently, Kang et al. adopted a simple bottom-up method to massively produce a microporous MOF \([\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{ndc})_{2}(\mathrm{dabco})]_{n}\) with diverse morphologies, including bulk crystals, nanocubes, and nanosheets, and incorporated them into PBI to fabricate a series of MMMs for postcombustion \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capture.[105b] Pure gas permeation data demonstrate that MMMs with MOF nanosheets exhibit the best \(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) separation performance owing to their increased tortuosity and large polymer–filler contact area formed by lamellar fillers. Moreover, it is of great importance to orderly orientate the 1D pores in 2D fillers toward the gas permeation direction in MMMs. For instance, Cheng et al. reported ultrathin MMMs containing 2D CuBDC nanosheets through a spin coating method.[39] The centrifugal force originating from the spin coating process helps to align these MOF nanosheets to selectively expose their pores toward gases, which largely enhances the membrane selectivity.

COFs have gained great interests as promising fillers in MMMs due to their pure organic nature that may confer them good compatibility with the polymer matrix. More interestingly, a large number of reported COFs possess 2D layered structures
that allow them to be exfoliated into multilayered or even single-layered nanosheets with high aspect ratios and intriguing properties. Pure ultrathin COF membranes have been proven possible by assembling exfoliated COF nanosheets in a simple vacuum-assisted approach.[106] However, these membranes exhibit poor gas pair selectivity (e.g., 4.2 for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\)), suggesting the existence of nonselective voids in stacked nanosheets. Zou et al. adopted a mechanical synthesis method to directly obtain COF nanosheets and incorporated them into polyether-blockamide (PEBA) matrix to prepare MMMs.[107] MMMs with only \(1\,\mathrm{\wt\%}\) of COF nanosheets exhibit a \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) selectivity of up to 72, which is \(70\%\) larger than that of pure polymeric membranes, highlighting the importance of additional gas adsorption sites created by COF nanosheets in final membranes.

# 3.2. Design of Polymer–Filler Interface

The poor polymer–filler compatibility is always a daunting challenge in MMMs. Considering the inorganic–organic hybrid nature of MOFs, this problem becomes even more obvious especially when dealing with isotropic MOF crystal fillers. The limited interactions between MOF crystals and the polymer matrix inevitably result in the formation of nonselective voids or cracks in MMMs, which eventually causes a huge loss in membrane selectivity. Surface modification might be an effective strategy to increase the polymer–MOF compatibility. For example, Wang et al. coated a thin polydopamine (PD) layer on nanosized ZIF-8 fillers (ZIF-8@PD), hoping to improve the polymer–filler compatibility with the assistance of hydrogen bonding between PD molecules and PI matrix (Figure 9).[27] The enhanced polymer–filler compatibility was confirmed by the improved mechanical strength of membranes after the addition of ZIF-8@PD, and no significant gas selectivity loss was observed even at a filler loading of as high as \(30\;\mathrm{wt\%}\). Similarly, Zhang et al. coated a thin and uniform PD layer on a hydrophilic MOF with sulfonic acid group \(\mathrm{(SO_{3}H{-}MIL{-}101{-}C r@P D)}\), and added these into hydrophilic PVA to fabricate MMMs for dehydration of ethylene glycol.[108] The hydrogen bonding between amine groups in PD and hydroxyl groups in PVA matrix greatly enhances the filler–PVA

compatibility, therefore promoting the formation of defect-free MMMs. Besides, Xie et al. designed \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) –UiO-66 MOF nanoparticles coated by poly(ethylene glycol) methyl ether methacrylate via strong covalent bonds \(\mathrm{P}@\mathrm{MOF}2\) and then incorporated them into a polymer matrix to yield MMMs.[109] Pure MOF and partially coated MOF nanoparticles \(\mathrm{P}@\mathrm{MOF}1\) were also investigated in MMMs as comparisons. At the same filler loading \((20\;\mathrm{wt\%})\), MMMs with \(\mathrm{P}@\mathrm{MOF}2\) show a higher \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) selectivity compared to bare polymeric membranes, while MMMs with either \(\mathrm{P}@\mathrm{MOF}1\) or pure MOF exhibit a sharply decreased selectivity owing to the existence of interfacial defects. Furthermore, MMMs incorporated with \(\mathrm{P}@\mathrm{MOF}2\) could be processed into thin films with separation performance attractive for economical postcombustion \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capture.

The incorporation of functional groups, such as \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), \(-\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}\), and \(-\mathrm{COOH}\) into MOF structures can facilitate the formation of hydrogen bonding at polymer–MOF interfaces, helping to mitigate the issue of compatibility. For instance, Xiang et al. prepared a series of novel ZIF-7–\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) nanocrystals with different ligand ratios by in situ substitution of 2-aminobenzimidazole into the ZIF-7 framework, and incorporated them into cross-linked poly(ethylene oxide) rubbery polymer to prepare MMMs (Figure 10a).[110] The as-prepared \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-functionalized ZIF-7 nanocrystals are denoted as ZIF-7–\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}(x)\), where \(x\) illustrates the percentage of 2-aminobenzimidazole to the total imidazole ligands in the crystal growth solution. ZIF-7–\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}(70)\) samples were found to be the optimum fillers in terms of their crystallinity and \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) functional group content. Contrary to the narrow pore configuration in the original ZIF-7, ZIF-7–\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}(70)\) possesses fully opened pores that permit the free access of gases, leading to an enhanced \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake capacity in \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) substituted nanocrystals (Figure 10b). Moreover, the chelating effect between metal nodes in MOF fillers and ester groups of the polymer matrix largely improves the membrane mechanical strength, as reflected by the \(874\%\), \(341\%\), and \(175\%\) enhancement in membrane tensile strength, Young's modulus, and breaking elongation, respectively, after the addition of \(18\;\mathrm{wt\%}\) of ZIF-7–\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}(70)\) fillers (Figure 10c). Finally, the incorporation of ZIF-7–\(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) fillers simultaneously makes the neat

