Thin-film nanocomposite (TFN) membranes, composed of an ultrathin separating barrier layer on top of a highly porous mechanical support, have gained great interests owing to their orders of magnitude higher permeance compared to that of dense MMMs. They can be facilely prepared by embedding porous fillers into a polyamide thin film by interfacial polymerization. Since the pioneering work of Sorribas et al. on MOF-based TFN membranes for the separation of methanol and tetrahydrofuran from styrene oligomers,[112] a great number of MOF and COF based ultrathin membranes have been prepared for diverse applications, including OSN,[113] desalination,[114] dye removal,[115] gas separation,[116] etc. Very recently, Xie et al. developed a bottom-up approach to prepare a novel type of MMMs by depositing an ultrathin \((\approx\!30\ \mathrm{\nm})\), continuous, and defect-free polymeric membrane on a coarse MOF gutter layer that grows on anodic aluminium oxide (AAO) supports (Figure 11).[117] Contrary to the dispersion of porous fillers in the polymer matrix in conventional MMMs, this novel ultrathin MMM intends to separate these two phases to minimize possible defects at their interfaces. The MOF layer functions as a mechanical support for the selective polymeric layer and creates high gas permeation channels due to its high porosity. As a result, this membrane exhibits excellent gas separation performance for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capture, offering an efficient universal strategy to fabricate high-flux membranes that are attractive for industrial applications.

# 4. Membrane Separation Applications

# 4.1. Gas Separation

Porous materials have been an inseparable entity in the fabrication of high performance gas separation membranes since the invention of MMMs. Advances in porous materials have resulted in the ever-increasing amalgamation of more exotic porous materials into the polymer matrix to form MMMs which may outperform their original polymeric membranes. The merits of employing advanced porous materials instead of traditional porous materials are higher porosity and improved

interactions between fillers and the polymer matrix due to the presence of functionalizable organic moieties. Generally, the gas transport mechanism of MMMs follows the classic solution diffusion model (Equation (1)) with the selectivity \((\alpha)\) calculated in Equation (2)

\[
P=D\times S
\]

\[
\alpha_{\mathrm{p}}=\frac{P_{\mathrm{A}}}{P_{\mathrm{B}}}=\alpha_{\mathrm{D}}\times\alpha_{\mathrm{S}}=\frac{D_{\mathrm{A}}}{D_{\mathrm{B}}}\times\frac{S_{\mathrm{A}}}{S_{\mathrm{B}}}
\]

where \(P\), \(D\), and \(S\) refer to the permeability, diffusivity, and solubility coefficients, respectively. Depending on the industrial processes where the gas pair will be separated, selection of porous materials to fabricate MMMs is of utmost importance. Based on Equations (1) and (2), improving the separation performance of MMMs can be done by increasing either the diffusivity-based selectivity or the solubility-based selectivity, or both.

\(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) separation lies at the center of the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) production process where \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) is produced as the main by-product, manifesting a separation market of up to US \(\mathbb{S}150\) million in the year 2020.[118] Recent studies have revealed that ZIFs possess a great potential for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) separation owing to their exceptional thermal and chemical stabilities coupled with a high \(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) selectivity and wide topological varieties.[12a,119] ZIFs are particularly preferred due to their relatively small aperture sizes so that the diffusivity-based selectivity of the resultant MMMs can be improved. ZIF-based MMMs for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) separation were reported by Ordonez et al. who incorporated \(60\,\mathrm{\wt\%}\) of ZIF-8 into the Matrimid 5218 matrix to primarily improve \(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) selectivity.[24] Further studies by Yang and co-workers and Sanchez-Lainez et al. revealed that integration of ZIF-7, ZIF-8, ZIF-11, or ZIF-90 into PBI or Matrimid 5218 matrix enhances both \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) permeability and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) selectivity at \(35\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and elevated temperatures.[120] In addition to ZIFs, limited varieties of other MOFs, such as \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}-\) MIL-53(Al) and \(\mathrm{UiO-66(Hf)-(OH)_{2}}\), have also been conveyed by Hu et al. and Perez et al., respectively, to simultaneously improve \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) permeability and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) selectivity.[70,121]

For other gas separating applications, such as \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}\) \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capture), \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) (natural gas and biogas upgrading), ethylene/ethane, and propylene/propane (olefin/paraffin separations), increasing solubility-based selectivity without dramatically compromising diffusivity-based selectivity has been regarded as a more promising approach for enhancing gas separation performance. The aperture size of MOFs may provide the minimum molecular sieving effect while the metal building blocks and the functionalizable organic moieties may increase the solubility of highly condensable gases such as \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\), ethylene, and propylene. Earlier studies on MOF-based MMMs to increase the solubility selectivity focus on ZIF-7, ZIF-8, ZIF-71, ZIF-90, MOF-5, \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}_{2}\), and MIL-53 (Al).[23a,34,101,122] It is interesting to note that among the widely studied MOFs, ZIF-7 possesses the smallest aperture size \((3.0\,\mathrm{\AA})\) which is even smaller than the kinetic diameter of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) \((3.3\,\mathrm{\AA})\). Nevertheless, it has been widely accepted that the organic moieties in ZIFs are flexible instead of rigid thus allowing larger gas molecules to be adsorbed into their pores; known as gate opening effect.[123]
be the next-generation membranes with high porosity (or low flow resistance), decent chemical resistance, good mechanical strength, and high permeability/selectivity that may go beyond the trade-off limits.

The application of MOFs for water separation is impeded by the limited stability of metal-containing SBUs in water. Typically, group IV metals (e.g., Ti, Zr, and Hf in the \(+4\) oxidation states) and ligands with low pKa values (e.g., BDC, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) –BDC) may lead to MOFs with high thermodynamic stability in water.[132] In 2015, Liu et al. fabricated pure-phase Zr-MOF (i.e., UiO-66) polycrystalline membranes on alumina hollow fibers using an in situ solvothermal synthesis method.[133] The resultant membrane shows excellent rejection to multivalent ions and no degradation in saline solutions for \(170~\mathrm{h}\) . Another study by Wang et al. attempted to seal ligand-missing defects in UiO-66(Zr)– \(\left(\mathrm{OH}\right)_{2}\) membranes by postsynthetic defect healing, leading to improved NaCl rejection.[134] Compared to polycrystalline membranes, MMMs have been more widely studied owing to their easy processability and decent separation performance. Zhang et al. fabricated ZIF-8/poly(sodium 4-styrenesulfonate) (PSS) MMMs via a self-assembly process (Figure 13a), and managed to obtain a water flux of \(26.5\ \mathrm{L\m^{-2}\ h^{-1}\ b a^{-1}}\) and \(98.6\%\) rejection to methyl blue (MW 319.9).[135] These membranes are stabilized by the coordination between the positively charged metal ions and negatively charged PSS polymers. When MOFs are introduced into the interfacially polymerized polyamide layer to form TFN membranes, increased water fluxes at similar salt rejections are found in both RO (with ZIF-8) and FO (with UiO-66) processes.[136] The hydrophobic ZIF-8 and hydrophilic UiO-66 MOFs are compatible with the polyamide matrix, and enable faster water transport within the framework.

COFs are emerging as a competitive candidate for water treatment with their high porosity, well-organized channel structures, and durability in water. It was reported that the continuous imine-linked COF–LZU1 membrane shows a water permeability of \(76\ \mathrm{L\m^{-2}\ h^{-1}\ b a^{-1}}\) and \({>}90\%\) rejection to dyes larger than \(1.2\ \mathrm{nm}\).[137] Wang et al. incorporated aminerich COFs into the interfacially polymerized polyamide layer, resulting in almost doubled water flux while maintaining \(80\%\) rejection to \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}\).[138] Valentino et al. developed an asymmetric thin film composite membrane with a COF active layer

by the interfacial polymerization between terephthalaldehyde and tris(4-aminophenyl) benzene monomers. A \(10\,\textrm{n m}\) thick imine-linked film was formed at the top which increased the membrane rejection to Rhodamine-WT.[139]

# 4.2.2. Pervaporation

Pervaporation has been established as a promising technology for the separation of binary or multicomponent liquid mixtures in the biorefinery, petrochemical, and pharmaceutical industries, such as dehydration of organic solvents, removal of organics from aqueous streams, separation of organic–organic mixtures, and reversible reactions.[129,130b] In a typical pervaporation process, one or more components in the liquid mixture are preferentially absorbed and diffuse through the membrane, evaporate at the other side of the membrane under vacuum and are collected as the permeate.

MMSs containing MOF fillers, especially the ZIFs and MIL series, have been extensively studied for the applications in pervaporation over the last decade.[140] For example, ZIF-90 nanoparticles with sizes of \(55\,\textrm{n m}\) were added into P84 for the dehydration of isopropanol.[140a] The flux increases with increasing ZIF-90 loading while the separation factor is maximal at less than \(20~\mathrm{wt}\%\) loadings. The ZIF-71/PDMS membrane shows nearly doubled separation factor and improved flux for the recovery of biofuels.[140b] When MIL-101(Cr) was loaded into PDMS and supported on polyvinylidene fluoride, the packing of PMDS chains was interrupted by MIL-101(Cr), resulting in increased fractional free volume and higher permeation flux for the desulfurization of model gasoline. One more example is the use of MIL-101(Cr)/polyimide membranes for removing water from the esterification reaction of acetic acid with ethanol to reach a higher conversion rate.[141] The membranes are more stable than HKUST-1/polyimide membranes for similar applications.

In a most recent study, 2D nanoporous COF–TpHZ was blended with poly(ether sulfone) (PES) to form hybrid membranes for dehydration (Figure 13b).[142] The COF nanosheets spontaneously migrate to the surface, resulting in graded membranes with COF enriched on the surface. The hydrophilic

