# 4.2.3. OSN

OSN is emerging as an energy-efficient approach to replacing the current distillation or solvent evaporation processes in pharmaceutical, fine chemical, and oil refinery industries.[143] It is a pressure-driven process that allows molecular separation in the organic solvent media for the purposes of concentration, solvent exchange, and purification. The introduction of advanced porous materials into OSN membranes may improve their durability in harsh organic solvent environment and potentially achieve high permeability and selectivity.

MOFs provide a versatile platform for OSN. In addition to high surface area and pore volume, their chemical structure, surface properties, pore size, and particle size can be readily tuned to meet the requirements of OSN applications.[144] Sorribas et al. fabricated TFN membranes containing a series of MOF nanoparticles [ZIF-8, MIL-53(A), \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-53(Al), and MIL-101(Cr)] with the size between 50 and \(150\ \mathrm{nm}\) through in situ interfacial polymerization on the top of cross-linked polyimide porous supports.[112] These TFN membranes showed dramatically increased solvent permeance compared to the pristine membrane, without sacrificing rejection. It was found that the porous MOFs provided preferential flow paths for the solvents, while the polyamide layer surrounding MOFs maintained a high solute rejection.

Super glassy polymers such as PTMSP are suitable for OSN applications, but are extremely susceptible to physical aging. By introducing hyper-cross-linked polymers such as polydichloroxylene (p-DCX), the longevity and solvent transport of the hybrid membrane can be enhanced. For example, Cheng et al. proved the concept by showing two times higher and more stable ethanol permeance over a \(500\ \mathrm{h}\) continuous operation of the PTMSP–p-DCX mixed matrix membranes.[145]

# 5. Conclusion and Outlook

With the increasing demand on membrane technologies for industrial separations, studies on fabricating high-performance MMMs have been flourishing over the past few years. Great efforts have been devoted to designing and utilizing advanced porous materials as fillers in MMMs. Those materials include extended frameworks such as MOFs and POFs, and discrete porous molecular additives such as POCs and MOCs owing to their combined properties of good affinity to the polymer matrix, high porosity, adjustable pore size and structure, and rich chemical functionality at a molecular level. These advanced porous filler materials naturally lead to several novel trends in MMMs, including the utilization of 2D fillers, polymer–filler interface design, and ultrathin MMM preparation, aiming at constructing economically attractive membranes with excellent polymer–filler compatibility toward industrial gas and liquid separations. Despite of recent advances in this field, MMMs with excellent separation performance, good mechanical

strength, high long-term stability, easy processability, and low cost have yet to be realized, indicating the coexistence of opportunities and challenges. Here, we list several aspects that are in urgent demand for improvement.

First, it is highly encouraged to perform more computational studies to predict specific polymer–filler microenvironment and reveal the permeation process of analytes through porous fillers in MMMs. MOF-based MMMs have been the focus of computational studies so far, where atomic simulations coupled with predictive models can provide effective tools to screen promising polymer–filler combinations.[146] However, current simulations often fail to precisely predict the existence of nonselective voids and filler agglomeration at high filler loadings \((>\!30\,\mathrm{\wt}\%)\) in MMMs.[164a] Besides, the basic assumption of rigid and defect-free framework models for fillers in MMMs may not be accurate, especially for some flexible frameworks or crystals with missing building blocks.[147] More importantly, it still remains a daunting task to increase the thickness of simulated membranes from atomic scale to nanometer or even micrometer scale. Therefore, great efforts are still needed to develop more advanced computational tools to further address these issues.

Second, advanced characterization techniques can be applied to MMMs for a better understanding on membrane structure–performance relationships. For instance, Raman spectroscopy can function as a nondestructive technique to probe the filler dispersion inside MMMs.[148] Nevertheless, it heavily relies on independent Raman signals from fillers and polymers, which are supposed not to disturb with each other. On the contrary, FIBSEM offers more visualized results by reconstructing 3D images of selected membrane area, providing quantitative information on the filler orientation and void fraction inside MMMs. More recently, infrared microscopy has been proven to be effective in monitoring the propagation of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecules during transient adsorption and their location, e.g., in filler, polymer, or interfacial region, at equilibrium.[149] This nondestructive and fast technique reveals that \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) molecules propagate from porous fillers to their surrounding polymer matrix, highlighting the significance of transport highways constructed by these porous fillers.

Third, it is challenging to design and synthesize novel advanced porous materials with size-selective pores or apertures, favorable geometry (i.e., nanoparticles, nanosheets), good chemical and thermal stability, and excellent polymer–filler compatibility. Considering that porous materials constructed from covalent bonds often exhibit enhanced chemical stability compared to their coordination-bonded counterparts, it is therefore anticipated that POFs and POCs will deserve greater research attention compared to MOFs and MOCs in the future. Moreover, their pure organic nature is beneficial for improving the polymer–filler compatibility, leading to the preparation of defect-free MMMs. However, current porous organic materials are still far from being ideal fillers in MMMs. For example, nearly all COFs exhibit pore sizes of larger than \(8\ \mathrm{\AA}\), almost twice of light gas diameters, preventing them from constructing size-selective channels in MMMs for gas separation.[12b] Postsynthetic modification or bulky monomer design may provide effective strategies to narrow their pores, which inevitably increases their cost and production complexity. As for POCs and MOCs, they often exhibit a pore aperture of higher than \(4\ \mathrm{\AA}\), which renders them unsuitable to discriminate light
